WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.600
How do the scientific facts about carbon and climate shape policy?

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.610
Carbon accumulates.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:08.710
Let&amp;#39;s think about the policy implication.

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:10.900
Let&amp;#39;s compare carbon emissions to air pollution,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.850
the other major impact of energy use.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:17.160
As we&amp;#39;ve discussed earlier, air pollution from energy use

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.740
has profound worldwide consequences.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.420
It kills several million people a year and shortens

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.790
the lives of people in the most polluted cities by many years.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:28.650
Yet the air pollution you breathe depends

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.850
on the emission of pollutants only over the last few days.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.640
If we could somehow stop emitting all these pollutants,

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.890
the air would be clean in weeks.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:38.950
Climate is not like that.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.040
Suppose we eliminated all carbon emissions today.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.750
Temperatures would continue to slowly increase and only

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.940
stabilize about a century from now.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.540
Sea levels would continue to rise for many centuries.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.170
Eliminating emissions will stop the main human force that drives climate change,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:59.160
but it will not make climate change go away, not for a long time.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.310
Carbon dioxide spreads globally.

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:04.090
So the climate impact of one ton of CO2 emissions

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:08.830
is the same whether it is emitted in Beijing or Rio de Janeiro.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.620
Air pollution is more localized.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.860
Some air pollutants have impacts over just a few kilometers,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.160
others over an entire continent.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.770
But collectively, air pollution risks are much more localized

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.330
than climate risks.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.650
These cold scientific facts have profound consequences

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.250
for climate policy.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.980
This is a big part of the reason it&amp;#39;s so hard for us

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.510
to get agreements to cut carbon emissions.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.440
When a country incurs a cost to cut air pollution,

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.830
it usually receives most of the benefits from the cleaner air

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.640
and gets those benefits relatively quickly.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.870
Contrast this to carbon emissions, where countries

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.870
may have to incur large cost to cut emissions,

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.360
and yet the benefits are spread over the whole world

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.730
and are not fully realized for centuries.

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.410
This difficulty is further compounded by the fact

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.850
that it&amp;#39;s cheaper to cut air pollution than carbon

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.330
and that countries have different contributions

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:01.230
to carbon emissions over time.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:05.780
For example, look at this figure showing current carbon emissions, flow,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:09.030
and historical contributions to the carbon stock.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:12.110
Notice that the countries with the largest historical contributions,

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.680
like the United States, aren&amp;#39;t necessarily the same

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.210
as those with the largest current emissions, like China.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.740
Both climate and air pollution are, in the language

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.880
of economists, public goods.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.200
It&amp;#39;s always hard for societies to coordinate to supply public goods.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.410
But it gets harder when the benefits are further away in space and time

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:36.070
and harder yet when the costs are high and the responsibilities diffuse.

